; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.17925v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Software Engineering\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 21 Apr 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:SpecAgent: A Speculative Retrieval and Forecasting Agent for Code Completion\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†George Ma , cite7†Anurag Koul , cite8†Qi Chen , cite9†Yawen Wu , cite10†Sachit Kuhar , cite11†Yu Yu , cite12†Aritra Sengupta , cite13†Varun Kumar , cite14†Murali Krishna Ramanathan L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled SpecAgent: A Speculative Retrieval and Forecasting Agent for Code Completion, by George Ma and 8 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite15†View PDF cite16†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at code-related tasks but often struggle in realistic software repositories, where project-specific APIs and cross-file dependencies are crucial. Retrieval-augmented methods mitigate this by injecting repository context at inference time. The low inference-time latency budget affects either retrieval quality or the added latency adversely impacts user experience.\nL17: We address this limitation with SpecAgent, an agent that improves both latency and code-generation quality by proactively exploring repository files during indexing and constructing speculative context that anticipates future edits in each file. This indexing-time asynchrony allows thorough context computation, masking latency, and the speculative nature of the context improves code-generation quality.\nL18: Additionally, we identify the problem of future context leakage in existing benchmarks, which can inflate reported performance. To address this, we construct a synthetic, leakage-free benchmark that enables a more realistic evaluation of our agent against baselines. Experiments show that SpecAgent consistently achieves absolute gains of 9-11% (48-58% relative) compared to the best-performing baselines, while significantly reducing inference latency.\nL19: Subjects:  | Software Engineering (cs.SE); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2510.17925 [cs.SE]\nL21:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2510.17925v1 [cs.SE] for this version)\nL22:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.17925†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: George Ma [cite20†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Mon, 20 Oct 2025 08:04:51 UTC (213 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Tue, 21 Apr 2026 17:40:07 UTC (269 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled SpecAgent: A Speculative Retrieval and Forecasting Agent for Code Completion, by George Ma and 8 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite15†View PDF L36:   * cite16†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite21†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite22†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.SE\nL43: \nL44: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L45: \nL46: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite28†cs L51: cite29†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite30†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite31†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite32†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: ### Bookmark\nL70: \nL71: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL72: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL75: \nL76: