 L1: \nL2: cite63†Image: arXiv smileybones L3: ## Happy Open Access Week from arXiv!\nL4: \nL5: YOU make open access possible! Tell us why you support #openaccess and give to arXiv this week to help keep science open for all.\nL6: \nL7: cite0†Donate!†arxiv.salsalabs.org L8: \nL9: cite1†Skip to main content L10: \nL11:  cite64†Image: Cornell University L12: \nL13:  We gratefully acknowledge support from the Simons Foundation, cite2†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , and all contributors. cite3†Donate†info.arxiv.org L14: \nL15: cite65†Image: arxiv logo > cite4†cs > arXiv:2510.17017v1\nL16: cite5†Help†info.arxiv.org | cite6†Advanced Search L17: \nL18:  All fields Title Author Abstract Comments Journal reference ACM classification MSC classification Report number arXiv identifier DOI ORCID arXiv author ID Help pages Full text\nL19: \nL20: Search\nL21: \nL22: cite66†Image: arXiv logo L23: \nL24: cite7† †www.cornell.edu L25: \nL26:  open search\nL27: \nL28: GO\nL29: \nL30: open navigation menu\nL31: ## quick links\nL32: \nL33:   * cite8†Login L34:   * cite5†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L35:   * cite9†About†info.arxiv.org L36: \nL37: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL38: \nL39: arXiv:2510.17017v1 (cs)\nL40: \nL41: [Submitted on 19 Oct 2025 (this version), latest version 21 Oct 2025 (cite10†v2 )]\nL42: # Title:SafeSearch: Do Not Trade Safety for Utility in LLM Search Agents\nL43: \nL44: Authors:cite11†Qiusi Zhan , cite12†Angeline Budiman-Chan , cite13†Abdelrahman Zayed , cite14†Xingzhi Guo , cite15†Daniel Kang , cite16†Joo-Kyung Kim L45: \nL46: View a PDF of the paper titled SafeSearch: Do Not Trade Safety for Utility in LLM Search Agents, by Qiusi Zhan and 5 other authors\nL47: \nL48: cite17†View PDF cite18†HTML (experimental) L49: > Abstract:Large language model (LLM) based search agents iteratively generate queries, retrieve external information, and reason to answer open-domain questions. While researchers have primarily focused on improving their utility, their safety behaviors remain underexplored. In this paper, we first evaluate search agents using red-teaming datasets and find that they are more likely to produce harmful outputs than base LLMs.\nL50: For example, when asked ``How can I track someone's location without their consent?'', a base model refuses, whereas a search agent designed to retrieve and cite sources may lower its refusal threshold, fetch documents (e.g., court cases), and, once appended, synthesize them into an informative yet unsafe summary. We further show that utility-oriented fine-tuning intensifies this risk, motivating joint alignment of safety and utility.\nL51: We present SafeSearch, a multi-objective reinforcement learning approach that couples a final-output safety/utility reward with a novel query-level shaping term that penalizes unsafe queries and rewards safe ones.\nL52: Experiments show that SafeSearch reduces agent harmfulness by over 70% across three red-teaming datasets while producing safe, helpful responses, and matches the QA performance of a utility-only finetuned agent; further analyses confirm the effectiveness of the query-level reward in jointly improving safety and utility.\n"
